Subject: base: compose a profile when the launch did not supply one, and refuse one that cannot be loaded

The loader's only source of a profile is --apostate-profile, so a binary run
with no arguments presents the host. That is the right default for a debug run
and the wrong one for the product: a user who double-clicks the browser expects
a device, not their own machine.

This is the one hunk that joins the two halves. The switch still wins, because
it is the strongest layer in the launch precedence and because patch 0005
propagates it to every child, so a renderer keeps parsing the switch its
browser was given and never composes anything itself. Only when the switch is
absent does the browser ask the compositor, and the compositor returns the same
base64 JSON the switch would have carried, so there is one parser, one format
and one profile object no matter which path produced it.

Composition goes through InstallComposedProfile rather than being done here:
composing and then writing the result back onto this process's command line is
one operation, patch 0084 needs the same operation from Chrome's startup
sequence, and two copies of it would be two places for the identity to be
decided. It is idempotent, so once 0084 has run this call finds the switch
already present and does nothing.

That is also the correction to this function's own comment. It used to claim
that initialising on first use "removes the ordering hazard entirely -- there
is no call site to place correctly, and no process type that can be forgotten".
The second half was true and the first was not: the identity was decided by
whichever subsystem first asked the loader for a field, which on Linux can be
after the zygote fork, and a zygote that caches a null profile hands every
renderer it forks the host's values. Patch 0084 places the call; this call is
what happens when nothing did.

The other half of this change is that a supplied profile that cannot be loaded
now stops the launch. --apostate-profile takes base64-encoded profile JSON, and
passing a path to a profile file is the obvious mistake to make, because the
file is right there and the switch names it. Before this, a path, malformed
base64 and a JSON array all produced byte-identical behaviour: one line on
stderr and then a composed profile, so the browser came up wearing a different
device and reported it as if nothing had gone wrong. A caller that built the
switch wrongly got a plausible wrong answer instead of a failure. An explicitly
supplied profile is the strongest layer in the precedence chain, so failing to
honour it is fatal and the message names the encoding the switch actually
wants. This cannot fire in a child process, where the value is a byte copy of
one the browser already parsed.

nullopt from the compositor still means host inheritance, and still reaches
exactly the code path that already existed. A child process, --fingerprint=off
and a catalogue that cannot serve the launch are all that path.

--- a/base/apostate/profile.cc
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.cc
@@ -7,8 +7,11 @@
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <cmath>
 #include <memory>
+#include <optional>
 #include <utility>
 
+#include "base/apostate/compose.h"
+#include "base/apostate/explain.h"
 #include "base/base64.h"
 #include "base/base_switches.h"
 #include "base/command_line.h"
@@ -87,23 +90,66 @@
     return g_profile_for_testing;
   }
 
-  // Initialised on first use rather than from an explicit startup hook.
+  // Cached on first use, but no longer *decided* on first use.
   //
-  // The command line is fully populated before anything in base runs, so there
-  // is no window in which this could parse too early. Doing it here instead of
-  // from content's startup sequence removes the ordering hazard entirely —
-  // there is no call site to place correctly, and no process type that can be
-  // forgotten. Function-local static initialisation is thread-safe.
+  // The profile for a launch is composed and installed onto the command line
+  // from a startup hook in chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc, which runs in
+  // the browser process before content forks a zygote or builds any child's
+  // command line. By the time anything reaches this function the switch is
+  // already present and this only parses it, so which call site happens to be
+  // first cannot change the identity. Function-local static initialisation is
+  // thread-safe.
+  //
+  // The composition call below is the fallback for a process that never
+  // reaches that hook: a base unit test, an embedder that is not Chrome, or a
+  // browser whose very first profile read somehow precedes PreSandboxStartup.
+  // It composes at most once per launch, because it installs the switch it
+  // composed and every later caller finds it. In a child process it composes
+  // nothing, which leaves the host inherited -- the same thing an absent
+  // switch has always meant. It passes no user data directory because base/
+  // cannot compute Chrome's default one; the consequence is that font
+  // provisioning cannot be checked on that path, and the composition reports
+  // it.
   static const Profile* const profile = [] {
     if (!CommandLine::InitializedForCurrentProcess()) {
       return static_cast<const Profile*>(nullptr);
     }
     const CommandLine* command_line = CommandLine::ForCurrentProcess();
-    if (!command_line->HasSwitch(switches::kApostateProfile)) {
+
+    if (command_line->HasSwitch(::switches::kApostateProfile)) {
+      const Profile* supplied = ParseOrNull(
+          command_line->GetSwitchValueASCII(::switches::kApostateProfile));
+      if (!supplied) {
+        // An explicitly supplied profile is the strongest layer in the launch
+        // precedence, so a value that cannot be honoured stops the launch. It
+        // used to fall through to composition, which meant a switch pointed at
+        // a profile *file* -- the obvious mistake, since the file is right
+        // there and the switch names it -- produced a working browser wearing
+        // a different device, and said so only on stderr. A caller that built
+        // the switch wrongly got a plausible wrong answer instead of a
+        // failure.
+        //
+        // In a child process this cannot fire: patch 0005 copies the value the
+        // browser already parsed, byte for byte. It fires in the process whose
+        // command line the operator wrote, which is the only one that can fix
+        // it.
+        RefuseLaunch(
+            "--apostate-profile is not base64-encoded profile JSON, so the "
+            "profile it names cannot be loaded and this launch stops rather "
+            "than composing a different device and presenting it as the one "
+            "you asked for. It takes the encoded bytes, not a path and not "
+            "raw JSON: --apostate-profile=\"$(base64 < profile.json | tr -d "
+            "'\\n')\".");
+      }
+      return supplied;
+    }
+
+    InstallComposedProfile(FilePath());
+    if (!command_line->HasSwitch(::switches::kApostateProfile)) {
       return static_cast<const Profile*>(nullptr);
     }
     return static_cast<const Profile*>(ParseOrNull(
-        command_line->GetSwitchValueASCII(switches::kApostateProfile)));
+        command_line->GetSwitchValueASCII(::switches::kApostateProfile)));
   }();
   return profile;
 }
